<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatali Khan of Quba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1736">1736</a>–<a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Fath Ali Khan of Quba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fatali Khan was Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a> from 1758 to 1789. Taking advantage of the break-up of Iranian power after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> in 1765 and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, Javad, Shirvan and other khanates his dependencies, uniting much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> along the Caspian. He sought Russian protection against his rivals, and after his death his realm fell apart among his sons and neighbours.</p></div>
